  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "SA-C20-man",
      "sequence": 960,
      "title": "नरः (naraḥ) — man, person",
      "headword": "नरः",
      "romanization": "naraḥ",
      "gloss": "man, person",
      "script": "devanagari",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:97dd0d702c7947c7",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Places, times, numbers — and nobody in them yet. This chapter supplies the people."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: नरः",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "नरः (naraḥ) — \"man, person, human being.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It is old enough to sit inside names you may already have heard: Narasiṃha, \"man-lion,\" and Nārāyaṇa. Its Greek cousin is anēr (genitive andros), the element inside English android and the name Andrew."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Ending in -aḥ, so you already know its family: masculine, like ग्रामः and मार्गः."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Your first person, and the ending told you what it was before the lesson did."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "naraḥ — \"man\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *naraḥ* — \"man\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "\"a good man\" — uttama naraḥ",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: \"a good man\" — *uttama naraḥ*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the English word built on its Greek cousin (android)",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the English word built on its Greek cousin (*android*)]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Which family does नरः (naraḥ) belong to, and how do you know? (Masculine — the -aḥ.) Which English words carry its Greek cousin? (Android, Andrew.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "SA-C20-woman",
      "sequence": 970,
      "title": "नारी (nārī) — woman",
      "headword": "नारी",
      "romanization": "nārī",
      "gloss": "woman",
      "script": "devanagari",
      "modality": "sight",
      "derivedModality": "sight",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "sight-cue"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:6e6f155cc31eaec0",
      "notice": {
        "modality": "sight",
        "needs": [
          "your eyes, because the lesson points at something written down"
        ],
        "waitUntilStopped": [],
        "text": "Before we start: this one needs your eyes, so it is not fully a driving lesson. You will want your eyes, because the lesson points at something written down. You can listen to all of it now and come back to the parts that need looking at once you have stopped."
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "And the ending you have not met yet."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: नारी",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "नारी (nārī) — \"woman.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Look at it beside नरः (naraḥ) and the relationship is visible: the same nar-, a different ending. Sanskrit builds a great many feminine nouns with a long -ई (-ī), and this is your first."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So the map has grown again: -am neuter, -aḥ masculine, -iḥ a third family, and now -ī feminine. Four endings, four families. You are not being asked to produce them — only to hear them, because everything Sanskrit does with a noun starts here."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "A second person and a fourth ending. The system is bigger than two, exactly as the night lesson warned."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "nārī — \"woman\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *nārī* — \"woman\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "it against naraḥ, and name the shared piece",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: it against *naraḥ*, and name the shared piece]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the four endings you have now met",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the four endings you have now met]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What do नरः (naraḥ) and नारी (nārī) share? (The stem nar-.) Which ending marks this feminine? (Long -ī.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "SA-C20-child",
      "sequence": 980,
      "title": "बालः (bālaḥ) — child, boy",
      "headword": "बालः",
      "romanization": "bālaḥ",
      "gloss": "child, boy",
      "script": "devanagari",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:af021472c9478897",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The youngest person in the chapter."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: बालः",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "बालः (bālaḥ) — \"child, boy, a young one.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "It carries a sense wider than English \"boy\": bāla is anything in its early state, and the word is used of young animals and of the newly-risen sun as readily as of a child. The feminine बाला (bālā) is a girl."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Notice that the feminine here is -ā, not the -ī you just met. Sanskrit has more than one way to make a feminine, which is inconvenient and true."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Three people, and an honest complication: the feminine endings are not a single rule."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "bālaḥ — \"child\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *bālaḥ* — \"child\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the feminine — bālā",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the feminine — *bālā*]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "the two different feminine endings you have now seen",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: the two different feminine endings you have now seen]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does बाल cover beyond \"boy\"? (Anything in its early state.) Which two feminine endings have you met? (-ī and -ā.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "SA-C20-old-person",
      "sequence": 990,
      "title": "वृद्धः (vṛddhaḥ) — an old person, an elder",
      "headword": "वृद्धः",
      "romanization": "vṛddhaḥ",
      "gloss": "an old person, an elder",
      "script": "devanagari",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:2966059227299b25",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The oldest person in the chapter — and a word that is careful in a way English is not."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: वृद्धः",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "वृद्धः (vṛddhaḥ) — \"an old person, an elder, one who has grown.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You already have पुरातन (purātana), which was glossed \"old — of a thing, not of a person.\" Here is the other half of that pair, and the distinction is the point."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "पुरातन is old as in ancient: it has existed a long time. वृद्धः (vṛddhaḥ) is old as in grown: from the root √वृध् (vṛdh), \"to grow, increase.\" An elder is not a thing that has lasted; an elder is someone who has grown."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "English flattens both into \"old\" and loses the compliment."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four people — and a pair of words that shows why a one-word gloss is sometimes a lie by omission."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "vṛddhaḥ — \"an elder\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *vṛddhaḥ* — \"an elder\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "which of purātana and vṛddha you would use for a temple",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: which of *purātana* and *vṛddha* you would use for a temple]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "what √वृध् means (to grow)",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: what √वृध् means (to grow)]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What root is वृद्धः (vṛddhaḥ) built on? (√वृध्, \"to grow.\") Which word would you use of an old building? (Purātana.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "SA-C20-people",
      "sequence": 1000,
      "title": "जनः (janaḥ) — people, folk",
      "headword": "जनः",
      "romanization": "janaḥ",
      "gloss": "people, folk",
      "script": "devanagari",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:48fcdcc3c2c01c60",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "All of them at once."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: जनः",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "जनः (janaḥ) — \"people, folk, a person, a race.\""
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "From the root √जन् (jan), \"to be born, to beget\" — the same root behind janma, \"birth.\" Its Latin cousin is gens (\"clan, people\") and genus, and through them English has genus, gene, generate, genesis and nation (from natio, \"a being born\")."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "So जनः (janaḥ) does not mean a crowd. It means those who were born — kinship rather than quantity."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Five people: man, woman, child, elder, folk — and every one of them arrived with its ending legible and a European cousin attached."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "guided-production",
          "title": "Guided Practice",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 1,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 1s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "janaḥ — \"people\"",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: *janaḥ* — \"people\"]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "three English words from its Latin cousin",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: three English words from its Latin cousin]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "prompt",
              "action": "SAY",
              "instruction": "all five people, in order",
              "spoken": true,
              "scored": false,
              "responseSeconds": 8,
              "source": "[YOU SAY: all five people, in order]"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 4,
          "type": "recall",
          "title": "Wrap-up Recall",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 3,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 3s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "What does √जन् mean? (To be born.) Which English words share it? (Gene, genesis, generate, nation.)"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
